# Copy to .env AT THE REPO ROOT (gitignored) — not supabase/.env. Per Supabase's own docs
# (Local Development > Managing config), the Supabase CLI reads env(...) substitutions in
# supabase/config.toml from a .env file at the project root, alongside supabase/, not from
# inside the supabase/ directory itself. This is separate from Edge Function secrets, which
# read supabase/functions/.env instead.
#
# Confirmed on this project (2026-09-21): if the variable is missing (no root .env, no
# exported shell var), the CLI does NOT error — it silently substitutes the literal text
# "env(VAR_NAME)" into the config value, which then fails later in a confusing way (e.g.
# `npx supabase start` erroring on a URL containing the literal string
# "env(SUPABASE_AUTH_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID)"). There is no built-in default/fallback syntax,
# so always copy this file before running any `npx supabase` command.
#
# CI does not use this file (a checked-in .env would defeat the point of gitignoring it);
# it sets the same fake default directly as a workflow env var
# (.github/workflows/supabase.yml) so npx supabase start works there too.

# Fake value: matches seed.sql's app_config.firebase_project_id and every pgTAP test's
# mocked JWT aud/iss (supabase/tests/*.sql). Leave this as-is for normal local dev — the
# app still runs, you just can't complete a real Firebase OTP login against local Supabase.
# seed.sql can't read this value itself (plain SQL has no access to OS env vars), so it
# stays hardcoded to this same literal on purpose — see the comment above the app_config
# update in supabase/seed.sql.
#
# To test a real Firebase OTP login against local Supabase instead, set this to your real
# Firebase project id (e.g. balajiinfraandhostel), restart `npx supabase start`, then run
# the app_config sync command documented in supabase/seed.sql so app_config matches —
# again, seed.sql does not pick this value up automatically.
SUPABASE_AUTH_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=balajiinfra-local-dev
